WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.336 --> 00:09.746 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:14.966 --> 00:15.926 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:17.106 --> 00:20.056 align:middle
Today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:20.096 --> 00:23.256 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:23.676 --> 00:25.846 align:middle
by 1/4 percentage point,

00:26.356 --> 00:29.636 align:middle
bringing it to 1-1/4
to 1-1/2 percent.

00:30.966 --> 00:33.586 align:middle
Our decision reflects
our assessment

00:33.956 --> 00:35.606 align:middle
that a gradual removal

00:35.606 --> 00:40.356 align:middle
of monetary policy accommodation
will sustain a strong labor

00:40.356 --> 00:44.736 align:middle
market while fostering a return
of inflation to 2 percent,

00:45.236 --> 00:47.596 align:middle
consistent with the
maximum employment

00:47.596 --> 00:51.356 align:middle
and price stability objectives
assigned to us by law.

00:52.376 --> 00:54.506 align:middle
Before saying more
about our decision,

00:54.506 --> 00:57.756 align:middle
I'll review recent economic
developments and the outlook.

00:58.816 --> 01:01.256 align:middle
Following a slowdown
in the first quarter,

01:01.586 --> 01:06.376 align:middle
economic growth stepped up
to a solid 3-1/4 percent pace

01:06.376 --> 01:08.796 align:middle
in the second and third
quarters of the year.

01:09.496 --> 01:12.876 align:middle
Household spending has been
expanding at a moderate rate,

01:13.506 --> 01:15.426 align:middle
business investment
has picked up,

01:16.046 --> 01:19.206 align:middle
and favorable economic
conditions abroad have

01:19.206 --> 01:20.696 align:middle
supported exports.

01:21.416 --> 01:23.886 align:middle
Overall, we continue to expect

01:23.886 --> 01:26.806 align:middle
that the economy will
expand at a moderate pace.

01:27.766 --> 01:31.416 align:middle
While changes in tax policy
will likely provide some lift

01:31.446 --> 01:36.326 align:middle
to economic activity in coming
years, the magnitude and timing

01:36.326 --> 01:38.236 align:middle
of the macroeconomic effects

01:38.556 --> 01:41.006 align:middle
of any tax package
remain uncertain.

01:42.106 --> 01:45.486 align:middle
Smoothing through
hurricane-related fluctuations,

01:45.886 --> 01:50.016 align:middle
job gains averaged
170,000 per month

01:50.076 --> 01:54.596 align:middle
over the three months ending in
November, well above estimates

01:54.596 --> 01:58.856 align:middle
of the pace necessary to absorb
new entrants to the labor force.

01:59.546 --> 02:01.886 align:middle
The unemployment rate
has declined further

02:01.886 --> 02:05.646 align:middle
in recent months and, at
4.1 percent in November,

02:06.046 --> 02:11.176 align:middle
was modestly below the median
of FOMC participants' estimates

02:11.396 --> 02:13.386 align:middle
of its longer-run normal level.

02:14.086 --> 02:18.826 align:middle
Broader measures of labor market
utilization have also continued

02:18.826 --> 02:19.646 align:middle
to strengthen.

02:20.476 --> 02:24.086 align:middle
Participation in the labor force
has changed little, on net,

02:24.366 --> 02:26.036 align:middle
over the past four years.

02:26.816 --> 02:29.146 align:middle
Given the underlying
downward trend

02:29.146 --> 02:32.896 align:middle
in participation stemming
largely from the aging

02:32.896 --> 02:34.526 align:middle
of the U.S. population,

02:34.526 --> 02:38.666 align:middle
a relatively steady
participation rate is a further

02:38.666 --> 02:42.116 align:middle
sign of improved conditions
in the labor market.

02:43.216 --> 02:46.366 align:middle
We expect that the job
market will remain strong

02:46.366 --> 02:47.436 align:middle
in the years ahead.

02:48.756 --> 02:52.196 align:middle
You may have noticed that we
altered the statement language

02:52.196 --> 02:54.016 align:middle
about the labor market outlook.

02:54.806 --> 02:58.066 align:middle
This change highlights that
the Committee expects the labor

02:58.066 --> 03:03.106 align:middle
market to remain strong,
with sustained job creation,

03:03.586 --> 03:07.236 align:middle
ample opportunities for
workers, and rising wages.

03:08.146 --> 03:10.526 align:middle
We anticipate some
further strengthening

03:10.526 --> 03:14.776 align:middle
in labor market conditions
in the months ahead; however,

03:14.776 --> 03:18.466 align:middle
we expect the pace of job
gains to moderate over time

03:18.896 --> 03:21.136 align:middle
as we gradually reduce
the degree

03:21.136 --> 03:23.446 align:middle
of monetary policy
accommodation.

03:24.506 --> 03:28.396 align:middle
Allowing the labor market to
overheat would raise the risk

03:28.446 --> 03:31.656 align:middle
that monetary policy would
need to tighten abruptly

03:31.656 --> 03:36.056 align:middle
at a later stage, jeopardizing
the economic expansion.

03:37.056 --> 03:40.146 align:middle
Even with a firming
of economic growth

03:40.146 --> 03:43.666 align:middle
and a stronger labor market,
inflation has continued

03:43.666 --> 03:47.926 align:middle
to run below the FOMC's 2
percent longer-run objective.

03:48.736 --> 03:50.816 align:middle
The 12-month change
in the price index

03:50.816 --> 03:55.286 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures was 1.6 percent

03:55.286 --> 03:57.946 align:middle
in October, up a
bit from the summer

03:58.006 --> 04:00.666 align:middle
but still below rates
seen earlier in the year.

04:01.346 --> 04:04.706 align:middle
Core inflation-- which
excludes the volatile food

04:04.706 --> 04:08.346 align:middle
and energy categories-- has
followed a similar pattern

04:08.686 --> 04:11.086 align:middle
and was 1.4 percent in October.

04:11.876 --> 04:15.466 align:middle
We continue to believe that
this year's surprising softness

04:15.466 --> 04:19.986 align:middle
in inflation primarily
reflects transitory developments

04:20.396 --> 04:24.206 align:middle
that are largely unrelated to
broader economic conditions.

04:24.896 --> 04:28.196 align:middle
As a result, we still expect
inflation will move up

04:28.196 --> 04:32.436 align:middle
and stabilize around 2 percent
over the next couple of years.

04:33.096 --> 04:37.016 align:middle
Nonetheless, as I've noted
previously, our understanding

04:37.016 --> 04:39.836 align:middle
of the forces driving
inflation is imperfect.

04:40.586 --> 04:44.796 align:middle
As emphasized in our statement,
we will carefully monitor actual

04:44.796 --> 04:48.106 align:middle
and expected inflation
developments relative

04:48.166 --> 04:50.306 align:middle
to our symmetric inflation goal.

04:50.866 --> 04:53.536 align:middle
And, as I've noted
before, we are prepared

04:53.536 --> 04:58.446 align:middle
to adjust monetary policy as
needed to achieve our inflation

04:58.446 --> 05:01.506 align:middle
and employment objectives
over the medium term.

05:02.546 --> 05:04.856 align:middle
Let me turn to the
economic projections

05:04.926 --> 05:07.756 align:middle
that Committee participants
submitted for this meeting.

05:08.576 --> 05:12.156 align:middle
As always, participants
conditioned their projections

05:12.396 --> 05:16.836 align:middle
on their own individual views
of appropriate monetary policy,

05:17.416 --> 05:20.836 align:middle
which, in turn, depend on
each participant's assessment

05:21.256 --> 05:23.786 align:middle
of the many factors
that shape the outlook.

05:24.406 --> 05:26.426 align:middle
The median projection for growth

05:26.426 --> 05:30.536 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product, or real GDP,

05:30.536 --> 05:35.896 align:middle
is 2-1/2 percent this year and
next and moderates to 2 percent

05:35.966 --> 05:40.426 align:middle
by 2020, a bit above its
estimated longer-run rate.

05:41.406 --> 05:44.296 align:middle
The median projection for
the unemployment rate stands

05:44.296 --> 05:47.676 align:middle
at 4.1 percent in the
fourth quarter of this year

05:48.256 --> 05:51.546 align:middle
and runs close to 4 percent
over the next three years,

05:52.046 --> 05:54.226 align:middle
modestly below the
median estimate

05:54.226 --> 05:56.086 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

05:56.736 --> 06:01.456 align:middle
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.7 percent this

06:01.456 --> 06:07.816 align:middle
year, 1.9 percent next year,
and 2 percent in 2019 and 2020.

06:08.516 --> 06:11.526 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in September,

06:11.776 --> 06:14.426 align:middle
real GDP growth is
a little stronger,

06:15.016 --> 06:17.146 align:middle
the unemployment
rate is a bit lower,

06:17.606 --> 06:20.206 align:middle
and inflation is
essentially unchanged.

06:20.986 --> 06:24.896 align:middle
Participants generally
identified changes in tax policy

06:25.326 --> 06:28.936 align:middle
as a factor supporting this
modestly stronger outlook,

06:29.406 --> 06:32.316 align:middle
although many noted that
much uncertainty remains

06:32.316 --> 06:36.466 align:middle
about the macroeconomic
effects of the specific measures

06:36.506 --> 06:38.546 align:middle
that ultimately may
be implemented.

06:39.596 --> 06:41.536 align:middle
Returning to monetary policy,

06:41.956 --> 06:46.126 align:middle
for the past two years the FOMC
has been gradually increasing

06:46.466 --> 06:48.946 align:middle
its target range for
the federal funds rate

06:49.386 --> 06:53.456 align:middle
as the economy has continued to
make progress toward our goals

06:53.456 --> 06:55.926 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

06:56.926 --> 06:59.986 align:middle
Our decision today
continues this process.

07:00.536 --> 07:03.436 align:middle
We still expect that
the ongoing strength

07:03.436 --> 07:06.426 align:middle
of the economy will
warrant gradual increases

07:06.426 --> 07:07.716 align:middle
in the federal funds rate.

07:08.716 --> 07:10.996 align:middle
That expectation is
based on our view

07:10.996 --> 07:15.646 align:middle
that this rate remains somewhat
below its neutral level--

07:16.576 --> 07:20.136 align:middle
that is, the level that is
neither expansionary nor

07:20.136 --> 07:23.696 align:middle
contractionary and keeps
the economy operating

07:23.696 --> 07:24.866 align:middle
on an even keel.

07:25.926 --> 07:28.286 align:middle
Because the neutral
rate currently appears

07:28.286 --> 07:31.066 align:middle
to be quite low by
historical standards,

07:31.516 --> 07:35.716 align:middle
the federal funds rate would not
have to rise much further to get

07:35.716 --> 07:37.776 align:middle
to a neutral policy stance.

07:38.656 --> 07:41.256 align:middle
But because we also
expect the neutral level

07:41.256 --> 07:44.896 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
to rise somewhat over time,

07:45.296 --> 07:49.226 align:middle
additional gradual rate hikes
are likely to be appropriate

07:49.226 --> 07:53.716 align:middle
over the next few years to
sustain a strong labor market

07:54.136 --> 07:55.696 align:middle
and stabilize inflation

07:55.696 --> 07:58.506 align:middle
around our 2 percent
longer-run objective.

07:59.286 --> 08:02.626 align:middle
Even so, the Committee
continues to anticipate

08:02.626 --> 08:04.506 align:middle
that the longer-run
neutral level

08:04.506 --> 08:08.726 align:middle
of the federal funds rate is
likely to remain below levels

08:08.726 --> 08:11.306 align:middle
that prevailed in
previous decades.

08:11.946 --> 08:15.786 align:middle
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

08:16.026 --> 08:18.136 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

08:18.836 --> 08:22.536 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate is 2.1

08:22.536 --> 08:28.806 align:middle
percent at the end of next year,
2.7 percent at the end of 2019,

08:29.236 --> 08:31.826 align:middle
and 3.1 percent in 2020.

08:32.576 --> 08:35.396 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in September,

08:35.866 --> 08:39.716 align:middle
the median path for the
federal funds rate is unchanged

08:39.716 --> 08:43.806 align:middle
through 2019 and a
touch higher in 2020.

08:44.016 --> 08:48.516 align:middle
I should note that the economic
outlook is highly uncertain,

08:49.016 --> 08:51.546 align:middle
and participants will
adjust their assessments

08:51.546 --> 08:54.156 align:middle
of the appropriate path
for the federal funds rate

08:54.746 --> 08:57.826 align:middle
as their economic outlooks
and views of the risks

08:57.826 --> 08:59.266 align:middle
to the outlook change.

08:59.926 --> 09:02.526 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

09:03.176 --> 09:06.356 align:middle
Additionally, the Committee's
balance sheet normalization

09:06.356 --> 09:10.046 align:middle
program, initiated in
October, is proceeding.

09:10.516 --> 09:14.376 align:middle
As we've noted previously,
changing the target range

09:14.376 --> 09:17.556 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is our primary means

09:17.556 --> 09:20.536 align:middle
of adjusting the stance
of monetary policy,

09:20.966 --> 09:22.746 align:middle
and we do not foresee a need

09:22.746 --> 09:26.246 align:middle
to alter our balance sheet
normalization program.

09:26.836 --> 09:30.656 align:middle
Hence, our statement no
longer mentions this program.

09:31.176 --> 09:35.156 align:middle
Of course, we would be
prepared to resume reinvestments

09:35.156 --> 09:39.256 align:middle
if a material deterioration
in the economic outlook were

09:39.256 --> 09:42.766 align:middle
to warrant a sizable reduction
in the federal funds rate.

09:43.706 --> 09:46.026 align:middle
Finally, I'd like to note that,

09:46.026 --> 09:49.266 align:middle
although I have one more
FOMC meeting to attend

09:49.266 --> 09:53.376 align:middle
in the New Year, this will be my
last scheduled news conference.

09:53.896 --> 09:57.446 align:middle
Over the next month and a
half, I will do my utmost

09:57.446 --> 09:59.626 align:middle
to ensure a smooth transition

09:59.946 --> 10:02.596 align:middle
to my designated
successor, Jay Powell.

10:03.356 --> 10:07.736 align:middle
I am confident that he is as
deeply committed as I have been

10:08.026 --> 10:11.426 align:middle
to the Federal Reserve's
vital public mission.

10:11.916 --> 10:15.326 align:middle
Thank you for being such an
attentive audience these past

10:15.326 --> 10:16.286 align:middle
four years.

10:16.286 --> 10:19.156 align:middle
And, as always, I'll be
happy to take your questions.

10:19.976 --> 10:21.336 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

10:22.036 --> 10:26.216 align:middle
I'm Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

10:26.576 --> 10:31.646 align:middle
Madam Chair, could you give us
any insight into the discussion

10:31.646 --> 10:35.306 align:middle
and how it dealt with
the major tax changes

10:35.306 --> 10:36.986 align:middle
that Congress is
considering now?

10:37.696 --> 10:41.456 align:middle
There have been thoughts that
with these changes happening

10:41.456 --> 10:44.236 align:middle
at a time when the
economy is already--

10:44.476 --> 10:46.106 align:middle
with unemployment so low--

10:46.146 --> 10:53.576 align:middle
that the Fed may be forced to
increase its pace on rate hikes.

10:53.956 --> 10:56.306 align:middle
Did any of that discussion
come up in your meeting?

10:56.886 --> 10:57.526 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

10:57.726 --> 11:02.386 align:middle
So, yes, we did discuss tax
policy, and let me say that most

11:02.386 --> 11:05.386 align:middle
of my colleagues
factored in the prospect

11:05.386 --> 11:08.206 align:middle
of fiscal stimulus
along the lines

11:08.206 --> 11:13.096 align:middle
of what's being contemplated by
Congress into their projections.

11:13.546 --> 11:15.306 align:middle
Now, I should emphasize

11:15.306 --> 11:18.656 align:middle
that some have been
incorporating those expectations

11:18.936 --> 11:21.946 align:middle
into their projections
throughout the year,

11:22.326 --> 11:25.166 align:middle
so changes to the
projections that you see

11:25.166 --> 11:28.976 align:middle
since September should not
be viewed as an impact--

11:29.266 --> 11:32.526 align:middle
an estimate of the
impact of the tax package.

11:32.856 --> 11:37.236 align:middle
And, in particular, broader
expectations of changes

11:37.266 --> 11:40.276 align:middle
to fiscal policy
have been reflected

11:40.476 --> 11:44.526 align:middle
in financial market conditions,
I think, over the past year.

11:44.526 --> 11:48.756 align:middle
For example, we have seen
a significant increase

11:48.756 --> 11:52.626 align:middle
in the stock market, and at
least some portion of that,

11:52.626 --> 11:58.176 align:middle
I would judge, likely partly
reflected expected tax changes,

11:58.466 --> 12:04.186 align:middle
and that effect-- along with
other financial market effects,

12:04.696 --> 12:09.446 align:middle
which affects, for example,
projected consumer spending

12:09.446 --> 12:11.546 align:middle
and would have affected wealth--

12:11.826 --> 12:12.856 align:middle
that's been part

12:13.276 --> 12:16.986 align:middle
of participants'
forecasts now for some time.

12:17.126 --> 12:21.776 align:middle
I think my colleagues
and I are in line

12:21.776 --> 12:25.536 align:middle
with the general expectation
among most economists

12:25.586 --> 12:29.486 align:middle
that the type of tax
changes that are likely

12:29.486 --> 12:33.836 align:middle
to be enacted would tend
to provide some modest lift

12:33.836 --> 12:38.136 align:middle
to GDP growth in the coming
years, and you see that--

12:38.386 --> 12:40.666 align:middle
in part, that's one of
the reasons, I think,

12:40.666 --> 12:45.426 align:middle
for the uptick you see in
estimated growth and decline

12:45.426 --> 12:48.386 align:middle
in the unemployment rate.

12:48.776 --> 12:53.086 align:middle
The views of participants, I
believe, have been informed

12:53.146 --> 12:55.586 align:middle
by a wide range of analysis,

12:55.586 --> 12:59.036 align:middle
including that of the
Joint Committee on Taxation

12:59.036 --> 13:03.156 align:middle
and other outside
evaluators, and my sense is

13:03.386 --> 13:05.866 align:middle
that their estimates
are essentially

13:05.866 --> 13:10.826 align:middle
in the same ballpark, although
they recognize, as I emphasized,

13:10.826 --> 13:15.896 align:middle
that there is considerable
uncertainty about the impacts,

13:16.226 --> 13:18.936 align:middle
and that will have to
be monitored over time.

13:19.536 --> 13:22.816 align:middle
More specifically, they
tend to see the package

13:22.816 --> 13:25.816 align:middle
as boosting both
consumer spending

13:26.046 --> 13:29.366 align:middle
and capital spending,
to some extent.

13:29.936 --> 13:36.456 align:middle
Now, to the extent that the
changes do have positive impact

13:36.456 --> 13:40.876 align:middle
on the growth of potential
GDP and longer-run growth,

13:40.976 --> 13:44.366 align:middle
let me just say that this is
something that, should it occur,

13:44.366 --> 13:48.256 align:middle
would be very welcome
to participants,

13:48.256 --> 13:52.446 align:middle
as long as it's consistent with
the attainment of our employment

13:52.446 --> 13:54.116 align:middle
and inflation objectives.

13:54.546 --> 13:59.226 align:middle
I guess I would also urge you
to remember that when you look

13:59.226 --> 14:02.416 align:middle
at the projections, that
there are many factors

14:02.416 --> 14:06.616 align:middle
that affect those projections,
and changes in tax policy--

14:06.926 --> 14:13.126 align:middle
that's only one of a number of
factors, including incoming data

14:13.126 --> 14:16.016 align:middle
that has, to some extent,
altered the outlook

14:16.016 --> 14:17.896 align:middle
for growth and inflation.

14:18.146 --> 14:21.836 align:middle
All of that factors into
the projections you see.

14:21.836 --> 14:25.486 align:middle
But I think, bottom line,
when you look at assessments

14:25.486 --> 14:28.776 align:middle
of the funds rate path,
participants continue

14:28.776 --> 14:33.086 align:middle
to see gradual increases
in the target range

14:33.126 --> 14:36.426 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate as being appropriate

14:36.486 --> 14:39.286 align:middle
to sustain a strong labor market

14:39.556 --> 14:42.916 align:middle
and bring inflation
back to 2 percent.

14:43.236 --> 14:47.886 align:middle
And, look-- importantly,
there is a lot of uncertainty

14:47.886 --> 14:52.036 align:middle
about what the likely effects
will be, and my colleagues

14:52.036 --> 14:55.656 align:middle
and I will be committed,
as always,

14:55.656 --> 14:58.156 align:middle
to evaluating incoming data

14:58.156 --> 15:00.626 align:middle
and altering the
outlook as appropriate.

15:01.856 --> 15:03.106 align:middle
HARRIET TORRY.

15:04.086 --> 15:06.106 align:middle
Harriet Torry with
Dow Jones Newswires.

15:06.106 --> 15:07.956 align:middle
You and others at
the Fed have said

15:07.996 --> 15:10.056 align:middle
that soft inflation
should be transitory.

15:10.056 --> 15:12.146 align:middle
Are you confident that that
will still be the case,

15:12.146 --> 15:14.006 align:middle
particularly regarding
wage gains?

15:14.046 --> 15:15.766 align:middle
They've been pretty
moderate in recent months,

15:16.206 --> 15:18.676 align:middle
yet the economy is growing
and confidence is high.

15:18.676 --> 15:19.856 align:middle
Is there something going

15:19.856 --> 15:21.626 align:middle
on in the economy that's
making it difficult

15:21.626 --> 15:23.006 align:middle
for businesses to raise wages?

15:23.006 --> 15:23.296 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:24.446 --> 15:30.976 align:middle
So it is true that incoming wage
data suggests only modest upward

15:31.076 --> 15:32.996 align:middle
pressure on wages.

15:33.926 --> 15:38.246 align:middle
That leaves me-- that's one
factor, along with the fact

15:38.246 --> 15:40.276 align:middle
that inflation remains low--

15:40.596 --> 15:45.116 align:middle
with feeling that even though we
have a 4.1 percent unemployment

15:45.116 --> 15:49.326 align:middle
rate, that the labor market is
not overheated at this point.

15:49.656 --> 15:51.206 align:middle
Remember, the modest pace

15:51.206 --> 15:55.876 align:middle
of wage gains also
probably reflects slow

15:55.876 --> 15:57.586 align:middle
productivity growth.

15:57.706 --> 16:01.336 align:middle
But when you ask me about the
outlook for inflation, you know,

16:01.726 --> 16:07.026 align:middle
I've talked in detail about
this in the past and recognize

16:07.066 --> 16:11.276 align:middle
that there is uncertainty about
what's holding inflation down.

16:11.666 --> 16:16.586 align:middle
But my colleagues and I continue
to believe that the factors

16:16.636 --> 16:20.066 align:middle
that are responsible this
year for holding inflation

16:20.066 --> 16:22.946 align:middle
down are likely to
prove transitory.

16:23.456 --> 16:28.006 align:middle
That said, we all agree that
our inflation objective is

16:28.006 --> 16:29.316 align:middle
extremely important.

16:29.706 --> 16:32.866 align:middle
We recognize that there's
been a prolonged shortfall.

16:33.246 --> 16:37.126 align:middle
This is a symmetric 2
percent inflation objective,

16:37.406 --> 16:39.296 align:middle
and we continue to indicate

16:39.456 --> 16:43.346 align:middle
that we'll be monitoring
inflation developments closely.

16:43.346 --> 16:49.096 align:middle
And so this is on the horizon
and recognized to be one

16:49.096 --> 16:50.866 align:middle
of the risks facing policy.

16:51.426 --> 16:53.356 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

16:54.976 --> 16:55.616 align:middle
Thank you.

16:55.616 --> 16:56.886 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

16:57.506 --> 16:58.576 align:middle
Chair Yellen, I wanted to follow

16:58.576 --> 17:00.976 align:middle
up on the question
about tax changes.

17:01.516 --> 17:04.316 align:middle
When you addressed it earlier
today, and when you spoke

17:04.316 --> 17:06.306 align:middle
to Congress last month,
you often describe

17:06.886 --> 17:11.196 align:middle
that you would welcome
higher growth in the context

17:11.196 --> 17:13.566 align:middle
of the employment and
inflation mandate.

17:13.566 --> 17:16.676 align:middle
And I guess I wonder how you
judge the major provisions

17:16.676 --> 17:19.016 align:middle
of the House and Senate tax
plans: the corporate rate cut,

17:19.526 --> 17:23.096 align:middle
immediate expensing of
big-ticket purchases,

17:23.096 --> 17:24.476 align:middle
new rates for pass-throughs,

17:24.826 --> 17:27.176 align:middle
temporary rate cuts
for individuals.

17:27.836 --> 17:31.136 align:middle
Do you see those, on balance,
boosting the productive capacity

17:31.516 --> 17:33.316 align:middle
of the U.S. economy as opposed

17:33.316 --> 17:35.996 align:middle
to simply increasing
aggregate demand?

17:35.996 --> 17:38.876 align:middle
And, related to that, how
would you view the benefit

17:38.916 --> 17:40.986 align:middle
of such tax changes now,

17:41.366 --> 17:43.506 align:middle
when the economy is
nearing full employment,

17:43.846 --> 17:45.976 align:middle
versus at earlier periods

17:46.416 --> 17:48.216 align:middle
when there was greater
resource slack?

17:48.286 --> 17:48.866 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

17:49.386 --> 17:51.116 align:middle
So I think my colleagues

17:51.116 --> 17:55.076 align:middle
and I mainly see the
likely tax package

17:55.076 --> 17:59.986 align:middle
as boosting aggregate demand,
but also having some potential

18:00.056 --> 18:02.146 align:middle
to boost aggregate supply.

18:02.146 --> 18:06.006 align:middle
So changes on the
corporate tax side--

18:06.086 --> 18:10.146 align:middle
the reduction in the
corporate tax rate, expensing--

18:11.446 --> 18:14.156 align:middle
will lower the cost of capital.

18:14.526 --> 18:19.296 align:middle
And while there are a range
of estimates and uncertainty

18:19.296 --> 18:24.106 align:middle
about how much stimulus that
will provide to investment,

18:24.856 --> 18:28.236 align:middle
in general I would see some
stimulus to investment.

18:28.706 --> 18:34.516 align:middle
In terms of aggregate supply
effects, a stronger pace

18:34.516 --> 18:38.016 align:middle
of investment could
boost capital formation

18:38.306 --> 18:43.016 align:middle
and thereby raise productivity
growth and potential GDP

18:43.016 --> 18:45.066 align:middle
or output to some extent.

18:45.476 --> 18:51.676 align:middle
Exactly how large those effects
might be remain uncertain,

18:51.676 --> 18:52.996 align:middle
but that is a channel.

18:53.326 --> 18:55.386 align:middle
And I suppose it's
also possible--

18:55.716 --> 18:58.546 align:middle
I'm uncertain how
significant this would be--

18:58.956 --> 19:04.346 align:middle
that lower marginal effective
tax rates for those groups

19:04.346 --> 19:07.796 align:middle
that will see them could
boost labor supply.

19:08.136 --> 19:11.756 align:middle
And, again, there are a range
of estimates in the literatures.

19:12.016 --> 19:17.756 align:middle
I indicated I think participants
have reviewed a number of pieces

19:17.756 --> 19:22.946 align:middle
of analysis, including the Joint
Committee on Taxation estimates,

19:23.196 --> 19:27.316 align:middle
and the many outside analysts
who have weighed in on this

19:27.556 --> 19:31.386 align:middle
and been influenced by
that kind of analysis--

19:31.486 --> 19:34.226 align:middle
but there is a good
deal of uncertainty

19:34.226 --> 19:36.976 align:middle
about what the impacts would be.

19:36.976 --> 19:40.826 align:middle
And, to the extent that
there are larger impacts

19:40.866 --> 19:44.766 align:middle
than those analyses
assume on aggregate supply

19:44.766 --> 19:49.316 align:middle
or potential GDP, in the
context of an economy

19:49.366 --> 19:53.446 align:middle
that has had disturbingly
low productivity growth,

19:53.786 --> 20:00.836 align:middle
that would be welcome and could
support faster GDP growth,

20:00.836 --> 20:03.156 align:middle
at least for some
period, without--

20:04.486 --> 20:07.176 align:middle
you know, without
creating a need

20:07.176 --> 20:10.606 align:middle
to tighten monetary
policy to offset that.

20:10.606 --> 20:14.246 align:middle
So there are potentially both
demand and supply effects here.

20:14.646 --> 20:18.026 align:middle
So, importantly, you really
don't, at the end of the day,

20:18.026 --> 20:22.226 align:middle
see very much change in the
federal funds rate path.

20:22.646 --> 20:27.256 align:middle
Participants do recognize that
the unemployment rate is lower

20:27.256 --> 20:31.606 align:middle
than their estimates of its
long-run sustainable rate,

20:31.606 --> 20:35.786 align:middle
so I think we are in the
vicinity of full employment.

20:39.866 --> 20:40.896 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

20:41.416 --> 20:43.626 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

20:44.006 --> 20:46.266 align:middle
You have mentioned that the
Committee thinks there will be

20:46.266 --> 20:47.546 align:middle
wage increases next year.

20:47.546 --> 20:50.116 align:middle
I'm wondering if you could
clarify if that is coming

20:50.116 --> 20:54.066 align:middle
in part or mostly from the
changes to the tax plan.

20:54.066 --> 20:57.596 align:middle
Is that what the expectation is,
would drive the wage increases?

20:57.596 --> 20:58.926 align:middle
And I was also wondering,

20:59.396 --> 21:02.536 align:middle
I've heard from so many
female economists in academia

21:02.536 --> 21:05.936 align:middle
and at the Fed about what an
inspiration you've been to them

21:06.366 --> 21:09.026 align:middle
and how melancholy they
will be to see you go.

21:09.026 --> 21:12.436 align:middle
I was wondering if you just had
any final words to young females

21:12.436 --> 21:16.036 align:middle
and minorities who may be
looking to enter economics

21:16.036 --> 21:18.806 align:middle
or a banking profession
and rise to your level.

21:19.096 --> 21:19.526 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

21:19.956 --> 21:22.746 align:middle
I'm sorry, remind me of the
first part of your question.

21:22.746 --> 21:23.146 align:middle
[Laughter] HEATHER LONG.

21:23.716 --> 21:24.956 align:middle
Wages. CHAIR YELLEN.

21:24.956 --> 21:25.756 align:middle
Wages, yes.

21:25.756 --> 21:26.296 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

21:26.296 --> 21:29.376 align:middle
Is the tax policy driving
wage increase expectations?

21:29.566 --> 21:30.736 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

21:30.736 --> 21:33.456 align:middle
Well, I think, generally,
in a strong labor market

21:33.566 --> 21:37.556 align:middle
where many firms are having
difficulty finding qualified

21:37.556 --> 21:41.226 align:middle
workers, we would expect,
just through normal demand

21:41.226 --> 21:45.116 align:middle
and supply channels, to
see some upward pressure

21:45.116 --> 21:46.776 align:middle
on wage growth over time.

21:47.116 --> 21:49.626 align:middle
And as the labor
market is tightened,

21:49.876 --> 21:55.256 align:middle
we've seen some very gradual
drift upward in wage gains.

21:55.466 --> 22:00.226 align:middle
They remain-- it remains at a
low level, but I would expect,

22:00.476 --> 22:04.896 align:middle
in the context of an
ongoing strong labor market,

22:04.896 --> 22:06.606 align:middle
to see some upward pressure.

22:06.776 --> 22:08.546 align:middle
And I believe that's
the main thing

22:08.546 --> 22:12.826 align:middle
that my colleagues
are factoring in.

22:13.336 --> 22:17.156 align:middle
On your question on advice
to women and minorities,

22:17.406 --> 22:20.586 align:middle
in the Federal Reserve,
my colleagues

22:20.586 --> 22:25.616 align:middle
and I are very focused
on wanting to see

22:25.616 --> 22:30.926 align:middle
and do what we can to foster
greater participation of women

22:30.926 --> 22:33.536 align:middle
and minorities in economics.

22:33.846 --> 22:37.996 align:middle
We would love to, if we could,
increase our hiring ourselves

22:38.236 --> 22:42.176 align:middle
of women and minorities,
and we see that both women

22:42.176 --> 22:47.136 align:middle
and minorities are studying
economics in disproportionately

22:47.436 --> 22:49.646 align:middle
and disturbingly low numbers.

22:50.606 --> 22:57.716 align:middle
Although the women in STEM
fields generally are about even

22:57.716 --> 23:00.406 align:middle
with men-- represent
about 50 percent--

23:00.406 --> 23:06.446 align:middle
in economics, women
majors constitute something

23:06.446 --> 23:11.016 align:middle
like 30 percent of
undergraduate majors,

23:11.266 --> 23:15.826 align:middle
and there is disproportionate
low enrollment of minorities.

23:16.086 --> 23:18.226 align:middle
I will just say, from
my own experience,

23:18.486 --> 23:20.806 align:middle
I think economics
is a terrific field.

23:21.176 --> 23:24.576 align:middle
I've thoroughly enjoyed
my career in economics

23:24.576 --> 23:27.016 align:middle
and think there are
many different paths

23:27.296 --> 23:31.276 align:middle
that people can follow that
lead to satisfying careers,

23:31.556 --> 23:34.286 align:middle
and that there are
very interesting

23:34.286 --> 23:37.166 align:middle
and important questions
that economics addresses.

23:37.436 --> 23:38.866 align:middle
And it is a great field,

23:38.866 --> 23:43.826 align:middle
and I would like to encourage
greater involvement and think

23:43.826 --> 23:45.876 align:middle
that people will
find that satisfying.

23:46.206 --> 23:49.796 align:middle
And, just in terms of the
kinds of research that's done

23:49.796 --> 23:54.666 align:middle
in the field, I think also a
greater diversity, more women

23:54.666 --> 23:59.026 align:middle
and minorities, may change
the focus to some extent

23:59.026 --> 24:03.416 align:middle
of the questions that
people choose to look at

24:03.416 --> 24:07.506 align:middle
and the analysis that they
bring and a range of thinking

24:07.506 --> 24:08.806 align:middle
that bears on research.

24:09.076 --> 24:11.106 align:middle
And all of that would be
a healthy development.

24:14.616 --> 24:15.586 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

24:15.586 --> 24:17.266 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

24:17.606 --> 24:19.906 align:middle
Every day, it seems, we
look at the stock market,

24:19.906 --> 24:22.686 align:middle
it goes up triple
digits in the Dow Jones.

24:23.176 --> 24:25.426 align:middle
To what extent are there
concerns at the Federal Reserve

24:25.426 --> 24:29.606 align:middle
about current market valuations,
and do they now or should they,

24:29.606 --> 24:31.576 align:middle
do you think, if we keep
going on this trajectory--

24:31.876 --> 24:34.276 align:middle
should that animate
monetary policy?

24:35.156 --> 24:37.516 align:middle
Finally, maybe as a sign
of what's been going

24:37.516 --> 24:38.716 align:middle
on with valuations,

24:39.596 --> 24:42.276 align:middle
this cryptocurrency
called bitcoin keeps going

24:42.276 --> 24:43.016 align:middle
up every day.

24:44.366 --> 24:46.796 align:middle
What is the policy
of the central bank

24:46.796 --> 24:49.606 align:middle
of the United States of
the introduction, use,

24:49.606 --> 24:52.626 align:middle
and incredible rise in
popularity of bitcoin?

24:52.666 --> 24:53.446 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

24:53.796 --> 24:57.986 align:middle
Okay. So let me start, Steve,
with the stock market generally.

24:58.346 --> 25:00.616 align:middle
I mean, of course the
stock market has gone

25:00.616 --> 25:02.316 align:middle
up a great deal this year,

25:02.766 --> 25:08.626 align:middle
and we have in recent months
characterized the general level

25:08.886 --> 25:11.696 align:middle
of asset valuations
as "elevated."

25:12.196 --> 25:16.336 align:middle
What that reflects is
simply the assessment that,

25:16.836 --> 25:21.256 align:middle
looking at price/earnings
ratios and comparable metrics

25:21.256 --> 25:27.476 align:middle
for other assets other than
equities, we see ratios that are

25:27.776 --> 25:31.606 align:middle
at the high end of
historical ranges--

25:31.606 --> 25:33.866 align:middle
and so that's worth
pointing out.

25:34.356 --> 25:36.516 align:middle
But economists are not great

25:36.516 --> 25:39.836 align:middle
at knowing what appropriate
valuations are.

25:39.836 --> 25:43.126 align:middle
We don't have a terrific
record, and the fact

25:43.126 --> 25:45.906 align:middle
that those valuations
are high doesn't mean

25:46.216 --> 25:49.056 align:middle
that they are necessarily
overvalued.

25:50.266 --> 25:53.636 align:middle
We are in a-- I've mentioned
this in my opening statement,

25:53.636 --> 25:56.216 align:middle
and we've talked about
this repeatedly--

25:56.216 --> 25:59.776 align:middle
likely a low interest
rate environment,

25:59.776 --> 26:03.216 align:middle
lower than we've
had in past decades.

26:03.216 --> 26:06.356 align:middle
And if that turns out to be
the case, that's a factor

26:06.356 --> 26:09.756 align:middle
that supports higher valuations.

26:10.046 --> 26:15.496 align:middle
We're enjoying solid economic
growth with low inflation,

26:15.856 --> 26:20.496 align:middle
and the risks in the global
economy look more balanced

26:20.566 --> 26:22.146 align:middle
than they have in many years.

26:22.676 --> 26:27.226 align:middle
So I think what we need to and
are trying to think through is,

26:27.466 --> 26:30.376 align:middle
if there were an adjustment
in asset valuations

26:30.786 --> 26:35.266 align:middle
or the stock market, what impact
would that have on the economy,

26:35.666 --> 26:38.936 align:middle
and would it provoke
financial stability concerns?

26:39.296 --> 26:42.246 align:middle
And I think when we
look at other indicators

26:42.526 --> 26:45.156 align:middle
of financial stability risks,

26:45.386 --> 26:49.586 align:middle
there's nothing flashing red
there or possibly even orange.

26:49.916 --> 26:54.316 align:middle
We have a much more resilient,
stronger banking system,

26:54.676 --> 26:59.646 align:middle
and we're not seeing some
worrisome buildup in leverage

26:59.646 --> 27:05.906 align:middle
or credit growth at successive
levels.1 So, you know,

27:06.096 --> 27:10.956 align:middle
this is something that the
FOMC pays attention to,

27:11.226 --> 27:15.096 align:middle
but if you ask me, is this
a significant factor shaping

27:15.096 --> 27:20.006 align:middle
monetary policy now, well,
it's on the list of risks,

27:20.006 --> 27:23.166 align:middle
it's not a major-- it's
not a major factor.

27:23.166 --> 27:25.246 align:middle
And then you asked
about bitcoin,

27:25.246 --> 27:29.536 align:middle
and there I would
simply say that bitcoin

27:29.536 --> 27:34.036 align:middle
at this time plays a very small
role in the payment system.

27:34.596 --> 27:37.316 align:middle
It is not a stable source of--

27:37.316 --> 27:41.006 align:middle
store of value, and it doesn't
constitute legal tender.

27:41.526 --> 27:43.836 align:middle
It is a highly speculative
asset,

27:44.316 --> 27:47.636 align:middle
and the Fed doesn't
really play any role--

27:47.846 --> 27:53.956 align:middle
any regulatory role with respect
to bitcoin other than assuring

27:53.956 --> 28:00.516 align:middle
that banking organizations that
we do supervise are attentive,

28:00.576 --> 28:04.776 align:middle
that they're appropriately
managing any interactions they

28:04.776 --> 28:07.786 align:middle
have with participants
in that market

28:08.056 --> 28:12.366 align:middle
and appropriately monitoring
anti-money-laundering Bank

28:12.706 --> 28:17.546 align:middle
Secrecy Act, you know,
responsibilities that they have.

28:18.416 --> 28:18.786 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

28:18.786 --> 28:20.236 align:middle
Has there been a directive,
or, more importantly,

28:20.236 --> 28:23.076 align:middle
has there been a directive
about bitcoin to the banks

28:23.286 --> 28:25.496 align:middle
and their dealings with bitcoin
from the Federal Reserve?

28:26.376 --> 28:26.686 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

28:26.686 --> 28:31.646 align:middle
I don't believe there's been
anything specific about that,

28:32.186 --> 28:36.066 align:middle
just, generally, banks
have Bank Secrecy Act

28:36.066 --> 28:39.456 align:middle
anti-money-laundering
responsibilities,

28:39.456 --> 28:44.706 align:middle
and this applies to bitcoin as
it does in every other realm.

28:48.076 --> 28:48.556 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

28:48.596 --> 28:51.226 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen,
Donna Borak with CNN.

28:51.556 --> 28:54.626 align:middle
To return back to the
prospective tax bill questions,

28:55.096 --> 28:56.026 align:middle
in your view at all,

28:56.026 --> 28:59.496 align:middle
is the Republican tax bill
an ill-timed fiscal stimulus,

28:59.686 --> 29:01.896 align:middle
and are you concerned
at all it will wind

29:01.896 --> 29:05.366 align:middle
up squandering the tools both
the Congress and the Fed have

29:05.506 --> 29:07.356 align:middle
when it comes time to
dealing with a recession?

29:07.356 --> 29:08.076 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

29:09.446 --> 29:13.886 align:middle
So, look, I will just say that
it is up to the Administration

29:13.886 --> 29:18.946 align:middle
and Congress to decide on
appropriate fiscal policy,

29:19.296 --> 29:22.926 align:middle
and our job is to
maintain our focus

29:22.926 --> 29:24.886 align:middle
on employment and inflation.

29:25.476 --> 29:30.076 align:middle
We continue to think, as you
can see from the projections,

29:30.496 --> 29:36.396 align:middle
that a gradual path of rate
increases remains appropriate

29:36.706 --> 29:42.306 align:middle
even with almost all
participants now factoring

29:42.886 --> 29:45.356 align:middle
in their assessment
of the impact

29:45.356 --> 29:48.396 align:middle
of the tax-- the tax policy.

29:48.876 --> 29:55.376 align:middle
You know, it is projected that
the tax cut package will lead

29:55.376 --> 29:59.056 align:middle
to additions to the
national debt and boost,

29:59.356 --> 30:05.046 align:middle
by the end of the horizon,
the debt-to-GDP ratio.

30:05.556 --> 30:07.916 align:middle
And I will say-- and
this is nothing new,

30:07.916 --> 30:10.256 align:middle
this is something I've been
saying for a long time--

30:10.646 --> 30:17.616 align:middle
I am personally concerned
about the U.S. debt situation.

30:17.616 --> 30:20.796 align:middle
It's not that the
debt-to-GDP ratio

30:20.796 --> 30:26.166 align:middle
at the moment is extraordinarily
or worrisomely high,

30:26.756 --> 30:29.766 align:middle
but it's also not very low.

30:30.456 --> 30:35.526 align:middle
And it's projected, as the
population continues to age

30:35.526 --> 30:40.236 align:middle
and the baby boomers retire,
that that ratio will continue

30:40.296 --> 30:43.236 align:middle
to rise in an unsustainable
fashion.

30:43.646 --> 30:45.806 align:middle
So the addition to the debt,

30:46.156 --> 30:48.886 align:middle
taking what is already
a significant problem

30:49.136 --> 30:53.326 align:middle
and making it worse, is--
it is of concern to me,

30:53.656 --> 30:58.586 align:middle
and I think it does suggest that
in some future downturn, which,

30:59.456 --> 31:03.126 align:middle
well, could occur just
for whatever reason,

31:03.426 --> 31:09.066 align:middle
the amount of fiscal space that
would exist for fiscal policy

31:09.066 --> 31:13.826 align:middle
to play an active role,
it will be limited--

31:13.826 --> 31:15.546 align:middle
may well be limited.

31:17.046 --> 31:18.926 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

31:19.026 --> 31:20.516 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

31:20.756 --> 31:22.276 align:middle
A couple of longer-term
questions.

31:22.276 --> 31:26.006 align:middle
First of all, midway through the
year you talked about the issue

31:26.006 --> 31:28.796 align:middle
of whether inflation targets
might need to be raised.

31:28.796 --> 31:31.016 align:middle
There's obviously been a lot
more debate in the Fed's system

31:31.016 --> 31:31.936 align:middle
since then about this.

31:32.446 --> 31:34.946 align:middle
It may not be an imminent
issue, but do you expect it

31:34.946 --> 31:36.676 align:middle
to be something the Fed
should be discussing

31:36.676 --> 31:38.606 align:middle
over the coming years
as some sort of change

31:38.606 --> 31:40.046 align:middle
to the inflation target?

31:40.536 --> 31:44.066 align:middle
Second of all, the
amount of stimulus tools

31:44.066 --> 31:45.726 align:middle
that the Fed may
have at its disposal

31:45.726 --> 31:48.546 align:middle
when the next downturn
strikes are fairly limited.

31:49.316 --> 31:52.496 align:middle
One of the incoming
governors, Marvin Goodfriend,

31:52.496 --> 31:55.056 align:middle
has talked about the merits
of negative interest rates.

31:55.056 --> 31:57.376 align:middle
I just wondered whether you
see that as a potential--

31:57.376 --> 31:59.396 align:middle
or at least in theory,
for the Fed

31:59.396 --> 32:01.256 align:middle
to consider should it
run out of other options.

32:01.466 --> 32:03.596 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

32:03.956 --> 32:09.726 align:middle
So right now the FOMC
is not discussing

32:09.726 --> 32:13.046 align:middle
or considering its
inflation target.

32:13.416 --> 32:17.326 align:middle
We do see inflation as
likely moving up to target

32:17.326 --> 32:21.236 align:middle
over the next couple of
years, and I would say--

32:21.236 --> 32:23.596 align:middle
you said our stimulus
tools are limited.

32:23.906 --> 32:27.146 align:middle
I would want to emphasize that
if there were a negative shock

32:27.566 --> 32:32.136 align:middle
to the economy, we do have some
scope to cut the fed funds rate,

32:32.136 --> 32:35.506 align:middle
and there are other
tools available,

32:35.736 --> 32:38.216 align:middle
ones that we have
used previously:

32:38.496 --> 32:40.996 align:middle
forward guidance
and asset purchases.

32:41.306 --> 32:42.946 align:middle
So I think we're not--

32:42.946 --> 32:45.676 align:middle
I wouldn't say that
we're out of ammunition,

32:46.066 --> 32:48.396 align:middle
but certainly it's
been recognized--

32:48.396 --> 32:53.996 align:middle
and I've emphasized myself that,
in the longer run, we may be--

32:54.316 --> 32:57.246 align:middle
and we'll have to see how
this works out, but we may be

32:57.246 --> 32:59.496 align:middle
in low interest rate
environments

32:59.496 --> 33:03.256 align:middle
where it could prove useful
to have additional scope

33:03.626 --> 33:05.806 align:middle
to conduct monetary policy.

33:06.126 --> 33:11.266 align:middle
And, in that context, I
think additional research--

33:11.306 --> 33:14.986 align:middle
the academic economists and
others are thinking hard

33:14.986 --> 33:19.416 align:middle
about what more could be done,
and I think these are matters

33:19.456 --> 33:22.666 align:middle
that are certainly
worthy of further study.

33:23.116 --> 33:23.906 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

33:23.906 --> 33:25.356 align:middle
So you do believe that
negative interest rates,

33:25.356 --> 33:28.126 align:middle
at least in theory, are possible
in the U.S. at some point

33:28.126 --> 33:30.016 align:middle
if it should be necessary--
at least in theory.

33:30.866 --> 33:31.246 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:31.466 --> 33:34.476 align:middle
So, I mean, on that
I would say we have--

33:34.676 --> 33:38.596 align:middle
that's not something that we
have studied inside the Fed

33:38.926 --> 33:43.066 align:middle
to any considerable extent
and haven't seriously thought

33:43.376 --> 33:48.986 align:middle
about using ourselves during
this last downturn and recovery.

33:49.386 --> 33:52.956 align:middle
We have watched what's
happened in other countries.

33:53.196 --> 33:54.626 align:middle
I think that's worthwhile.

33:54.936 --> 34:01.286 align:middle
And I would say it's an area
that academics may have interest

34:01.286 --> 34:04.306 align:middle
in in the future and is
worthy of some study,

34:04.356 --> 34:07.226 align:middle
but it hasn't been part
of the Fed's agenda.

34:08.586 --> 34:09.836 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

34:10.596 --> 34:11.976 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen.

34:12.556 --> 34:14.136 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

34:14.196 --> 34:16.826 align:middle
I just wanted to ask you
quickly, on the regulatory side,

34:17.816 --> 34:20.856 align:middle
in your testimony to Congress
recently you spoke positively

34:20.856 --> 34:24.876 align:middle
about the Senate bank regulatory
reform bill, and I was wondering

34:24.906 --> 34:27.026 align:middle
if there are-- beyond
that bill--

34:27.026 --> 34:29.076 align:middle
if there are tweaks
to Dodd-Frank

34:29.126 --> 34:32.236 align:middle
that you think might
still be beneficial.

34:32.816 --> 34:36.556 align:middle
And then, somewhat related,
Governor Powell recently said

34:36.556 --> 34:39.806 align:middle
that there are currently no U.S.
banks that are too big to fail.

34:40.086 --> 34:43.316 align:middle
And I was wondering, do you
agree with that assessment?

34:43.316 --> 34:43.636 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

34:44.376 --> 34:48.966 align:middle
Well, I mean, you know,
I think I'm not familiar

34:48.966 --> 34:50.746 align:middle
with every detail of the bill,

34:50.746 --> 34:54.116 align:middle
but I think the bill
does address a wide range

34:54.116 --> 34:57.736 align:middle
of issues that, you know,
we've highlighted in the past

34:57.736 --> 35:02.076 align:middle
as being ones where perhaps
additional flexibility

35:02.486 --> 35:06.306 align:middle
to tailor our supervisory
requirements would

35:06.306 --> 35:07.306 align:middle
be worthwhile.

35:07.656 --> 35:09.866 align:middle
So I don't have additional
things

35:09.866 --> 35:12.086 align:middle
on the congressional list.

35:12.446 --> 35:14.876 align:middle
Sorry, and what-- the other
thing-- VICTORIA GUIDA.

35:15.326 --> 35:15.756 align:middle
Too big to fail.

35:15.756 --> 35:16.186 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

35:16.956 --> 35:17.726 align:middle
Oh, too big to fail.

35:17.906 --> 35:18.346 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

35:18.346 --> 35:20.056 align:middle
Are there any banks that are
too big to fail right now?

35:20.566 --> 35:20.926 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

35:21.336 --> 35:25.756 align:middle
So, you know, we continue to
work seriously on resolution

35:25.756 --> 35:32.566 align:middle
and the resolution plans, the
living wills, and the structure

35:32.566 --> 35:37.336 align:middle
of systemic firms to ensure
that it would be possible

35:37.446 --> 35:43.376 align:middle
to resolve a firm-- under the
bankruptcy code would be the top

35:43.536 --> 35:46.536 align:middle
choice of methods,
or, alternatively,

35:46.536 --> 35:49.846 align:middle
under the Orderly
Liquidation Authority.

35:50.166 --> 35:53.016 align:middle
And I think it's fair
to say that, over time,

35:53.016 --> 35:55.176 align:middle
we have learned more ourselves

35:55.176 --> 35:59.916 align:middle
and more clearly detailed our
expectations for the firms

35:59.916 --> 36:01.396 align:middle
that file living wills.

36:01.646 --> 36:06.086 align:middle
And the firms themselves have
made considerable progress in,

36:07.516 --> 36:09.046 align:middle
you know, changing what they do,

36:09.046 --> 36:12.726 align:middle
whether it's adopting
financial contracts

36:12.726 --> 36:16.756 align:middle
that would facilitate
a resolution rather

36:16.806 --> 36:20.376 align:middle
than a disorderly
unwinding of contracts,

36:20.866 --> 36:24.366 align:middle
making sure that they're
appropriately dealing

36:24.366 --> 36:30.066 align:middle
with shared services so that
key services would be able

36:30.146 --> 36:34.036 align:middle
to continue, governance
arrangements,

36:34.576 --> 36:40.826 align:middle
legal entity structures-- the
firms have all made progress

36:40.826 --> 36:44.406 align:middle
in adapting to our expectations

36:44.406 --> 36:48.996 align:middle
of what would enable a
successful resolution.

36:49.496 --> 36:52.696 align:middle
So I think it's an
ongoing process,

36:52.696 --> 36:55.366 align:middle
and I believe we have
made substantial progress.

36:56.576 --> 36:57.886 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

36:59.506 --> 37:00.936 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

37:01.336 --> 37:04.876 align:middle
I'm struggling to reconcile the
pieces of the economic outlook

37:04.876 --> 37:05.936 align:middle
that you've described today.

37:06.156 --> 37:08.686 align:middle
You've said that we are
basically at full employment.

37:09.156 --> 37:11.166 align:middle
You've said that you're
expecting a tax package

37:11.166 --> 37:13.646 align:middle
to deliver a significant
stimulus,

37:13.646 --> 37:16.956 align:middle
and that it will be
on the demand side.

37:17.076 --> 37:18.586 align:middle
You expect growth to be faster.

37:18.586 --> 37:20.666 align:middle
You expect unemployment
to be lower,

37:20.666 --> 37:23.716 align:middle
and yet somehow inflation is
going to remain at 2 percent

37:23.716 --> 37:24.936 align:middle
for the foreseeable future.

37:25.356 --> 37:27.136 align:middle
Could you describe
what has changed

37:27.136 --> 37:28.506 align:middle
about your economic assessment

37:28.776 --> 37:30.946 align:middle
so that everything has
changed except inflation?

37:31.266 --> 37:31.516 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

37:32.166 --> 37:37.606 align:middle
Well, you know, the projections
that we're showing you today,

37:38.346 --> 37:43.916 align:middle
many factors went into that,
fiscal policy being one

37:43.916 --> 37:46.426 align:middle
of a number of different
factors.

37:46.796 --> 37:52.076 align:middle
And, you know, you're
looking at 16 participants

37:52.276 --> 37:55.966 align:middle
who have made adjustments
to their economic outlook

37:56.266 --> 38:01.046 align:middle
for a whole variety of reasons,
including, in some cases,

38:01.296 --> 38:04.346 align:middle
rethinking some of the
fundamentals that went

38:04.346 --> 38:07.626 align:middle
into their original forecast.

38:07.626 --> 38:12.206 align:middle
So there have been modifications
in different directions

38:12.286 --> 38:13.976 align:middle
by different participants.

38:14.236 --> 38:20.506 align:middle
So I would caution you about the
dangers of looking at the median

38:20.506 --> 38:23.306 align:middle
and acting like that
is one individual

38:23.306 --> 38:27.696 align:middle
who has made a change
to their forecast.

38:27.696 --> 38:31.156 align:middle
That's a leap that
isn't quite justified.

38:31.226 --> 38:37.556 align:middle
But, look, generally, you
see modestly faster growth

38:37.556 --> 38:41.806 align:middle
over the next couple of years,
which is consistent and, I said,

38:42.116 --> 38:48.046 align:middle
I think for most participants,
reflects partly an impact

38:48.046 --> 38:53.756 align:middle
from taxes stimulating consumer
and investment spending.

38:54.046 --> 38:59.336 align:middle
But it's not a gigantic
increase in growth that since--

38:59.756 --> 39:01.356 align:middle
relative to September.

39:03.366 --> 39:07.466 align:middle
You do see a lower path for
unemployment, but remember

39:07.466 --> 39:11.336 align:middle
that inflation has
also been running low

39:11.336 --> 39:16.356 align:middle
on a persistent basis, and the
Committee does have a concern

39:16.356 --> 39:19.266 align:middle
about inflation and wants
to see it moving up.

39:19.756 --> 39:26.126 align:middle
And, on balance, you see only
modest changes, slight revisions

39:26.426 --> 39:28.556 align:middle
to the path for the
fed funds rate.

39:28.946 --> 39:31.656 align:middle
You might think, well,
shouldn't I see more?

39:31.656 --> 39:34.806 align:middle
Well, okay, growth
is a little stronger,

39:34.806 --> 39:37.556 align:middle
the unemployment rate
runs a little bit lower--

39:37.556 --> 39:40.316 align:middle
that would perhaps
push in the direction

39:40.316 --> 39:43.786 align:middle
of slightly tighter
monetary policy.

39:44.126 --> 39:47.726 align:middle
But, again, counterbalancing
that is

39:47.726 --> 39:52.186 align:middle
that inflation has run lower
than we expect, and, you know,

39:52.666 --> 39:56.706 align:middle
it could take a longer period
of a very strong labor market

39:56.706 --> 39:59.586 align:middle
in order to achieve the
inflation objective.

40:00.086 --> 40:02.666 align:middle
JUSTINE UNDERHILL.

40:04.146 --> 40:05.826 align:middle
Justine Underhill,
Yahoo Finance.

40:06.276 --> 40:08.526 align:middle
So William Dudley of the
New York Fed recently said

40:08.526 --> 40:10.676 align:middle
that the Fed is exploring
the idea

40:10.676 --> 40:13.076 align:middle
of potentially having
its own cryptocurrency

40:13.076 --> 40:14.106 align:middle
or digital currency.

40:15.086 --> 40:17.686 align:middle
What use do you see a
cryptocurrency could have

40:17.686 --> 40:20.786 align:middle
for the Fed, and do you see the
potential of a cryptocurrency

40:20.786 --> 40:22.436 align:middle
that might be considered
legal tender?

40:23.376 --> 40:23.806 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:24.026 --> 40:25.906 align:middle
So I want to distinguish
carefully

40:25.906 --> 40:29.126 align:middle
between digital currency
and cryptocurrency.

40:29.126 --> 40:35.386 align:middle
This is-- there is a discussion
going on among central bankers

40:35.596 --> 40:39.716 align:middle
about the potential
merits of adopting--

40:39.716 --> 40:43.736 align:middle
a central bank itself
adopting a digital currency.

40:44.036 --> 40:48.146 align:middle
And there might even be
a central banker or two

40:48.346 --> 40:51.306 align:middle
around the globe that
might go in that direction.

40:51.686 --> 40:54.876 align:middle
But I really want to caution

40:54.876 --> 40:59.186 align:middle
that this is not something the
Federal Reserve is seriously

40:59.186 --> 41:02.246 align:middle
considering at this
stage while we're looking

41:02.246 --> 41:05.406 align:middle
at research on this topic.

41:05.906 --> 41:12.596 align:middle
There are, I think, to
my mind, limited benefits

41:12.596 --> 41:16.406 align:middle
from introducing it,
a limited need for it,

41:16.406 --> 41:19.426 align:middle
and some substantial concerns.

41:19.426 --> 41:22.376 align:middle
And so I would really doubt

41:22.376 --> 41:26.056 align:middle
that the Federal Reserve would
soon go in that direction.

41:26.366 --> 41:30.346 align:middle
But it is something that central
banks are looking at to see

41:30.776 --> 41:33.586 align:middle
if there could be
benefits from doing it.

41:37.006 --> 41:38.246 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

41:38.246 --> 41:40.236 align:middle
Chair Yellen, before
I ask this question,

41:40.236 --> 41:42.836 align:middle
thanks for your stewardship
of the economy, the benefits

41:42.836 --> 41:45.866 align:middle
of which I've seen in some
of the harder-hit parts

41:45.866 --> 41:47.426 align:middle
of my own family,
so-- CHAIR YELLEN.

41:47.426 --> 41:47.606 align:middle
Thank you.

41:47.606 --> 41:48.326 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

41:49.916 --> 41:50.936 align:middle
Your tenure.

41:50.936 --> 41:54.986 align:middle
We've seen six Federal
Reserve presidents appointed.

41:55.166 --> 41:58.726 align:middle
One was a woman and five are
men, so I'd like to know how

41:58.726 --> 42:01.606 align:middle
that comports with your
commitment to gender diversity.

42:02.516 --> 42:05.386 align:middle
And, second, I wonder if
you think this process

42:05.386 --> 42:07.726 align:middle
of appointing Reserve
Bank presidents needs

42:07.726 --> 42:09.866 align:middle
to be more transparent
and accountable.

42:10.596 --> 42:14.196 align:middle
Recently, the Richmond Fed board
took 11 months, and they came

42:14.196 --> 42:19.276 align:middle
up with a candidate who was
another Fed board member.

42:19.576 --> 42:22.716 align:middle
And so I'm wondering what you
would say to critics who say

42:22.716 --> 42:26.876 align:middle
that looks like cronyism,
and if you think, you know,

42:27.346 --> 42:29.796 align:middle
this could be simply
a more open process.

42:29.926 --> 42:32.976 align:middle
These are votes on national
monetary policy, after all.

42:33.886 --> 42:34.296 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

42:34.296 --> 42:35.776 align:middle
So I would agree with you

42:35.776 --> 42:37.936 align:middle
that these are important
appointments.

42:38.416 --> 42:43.106 align:middle
Under the Federal Reserve
Act, the nonbanking directors

42:43.446 --> 42:46.436 align:middle
of the Reserve Banks
have responsibility

42:46.716 --> 42:50.866 align:middle
for conducting a search, and
the Board of Governors has

42:50.866 --> 42:53.076 align:middle
to sign off on the candidates.

42:53.716 --> 42:55.596 align:middle
We've made very clear

42:55.596 --> 43:01.886 align:middle
and we monitor ongoing searches
very carefully to make sure

43:01.886 --> 43:08.426 align:middle
that absolutely every effort
is made to create a pool

43:08.426 --> 43:15.196 align:middle
that is diverse, and that
there be a national search,

43:15.556 --> 43:20.956 align:middle
and that every attempt should be
made to create a diverse pool.

43:21.566 --> 43:27.196 align:middle
At the end of the day, we cannot
guarantee that the outcomes

43:27.196 --> 43:30.716 align:middle
of these searches will result
in an increase in diversity.

43:31.126 --> 43:33.166 align:middle
I mean, I've been
very pleased to see

43:33.166 --> 43:36.296 align:middle
that there's been some
success in that regard,

43:36.746 --> 43:42.066 align:middle
but we have signed off on the
individuals who were appointed

43:42.386 --> 43:47.256 align:middle
and have held the view
that these are individuals

43:47.256 --> 43:51.016 align:middle
who were qualified to
serve in these positions.

43:51.426 --> 43:57.716 align:middle
So I would hope-- I would
hope to see greater diversity.

43:57.716 --> 44:01.496 align:middle
It has been a challenge
to achieve that.

44:01.496 --> 44:02.616 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

44:02.896 --> 44:04.566 align:middle
Should the process
be more open somehow?

44:04.566 --> 44:04.806 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

44:05.116 --> 44:07.666 align:middle
Well, I think the process
has become more open,

44:08.026 --> 44:13.076 align:middle
and in a number of the recent
searches there has been

44:13.266 --> 44:17.746 align:middle
outreach-- public outreach,
information on websites,

44:17.746 --> 44:22.036 align:middle
acceptance of potential
names and nominees,

44:22.036 --> 44:25.066 align:middle
and those have gotten
careful consideration.

44:25.446 --> 44:29.256 align:middle
There has been-- there have
been meetings in some cases

44:29.256 --> 44:34.956 align:middle
with community groups to
try to enlarge the pool

44:34.956 --> 44:39.346 align:middle
and get suggestions, and I
think that's appropriate,

44:39.346 --> 44:42.496 align:middle
and I think it has moved in the
direction you're suggesting.

44:44.196 --> 44:45.376 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

44:45.376 --> 44:47.286 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

44:47.646 --> 44:49.436 align:middle
I'm wondering if
you have any advice

44:49.436 --> 44:50.996 align:middle
for incoming Chair Powell?

44:51.866 --> 44:52.786 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

44:53.646 --> 44:56.606 align:middle
Well, my colleague-- I've
had the pleasure of working

44:56.606 --> 45:00.896 align:middle
with him now for many
years, and he is somebody

45:00.896 --> 45:04.076 align:middle
who understands the
Federal Reserve very well

45:04.376 --> 45:06.106 align:middle
and shares its values.

45:06.106 --> 45:10.886 align:middle
And as I mentioned in my opening
statement, he is committed

45:11.076 --> 45:15.316 align:middle
to the mission of the
Fed; to its independence;

45:15.316 --> 45:20.106 align:middle
and to its acting in a
nonpartisan, nonpolitical way.

45:21.176 --> 45:24.636 align:middle
He is somebody who
has participated

45:24.636 --> 45:27.896 align:middle
in the FOMC now for many years.

45:28.176 --> 45:33.596 align:middle
To the best of my knowledge, he
has been part of the consensus.

45:33.596 --> 45:35.766 align:middle
I don't believe he's
ever dissented.

45:36.186 --> 45:40.376 align:middle
I think there is strong
consensus in the Committee

45:41.246 --> 45:45.346 align:middle
for the gradual approach
that we have been pursuing,

45:45.696 --> 45:49.076 align:middle
and Governor Powell has
been part of that consensus,

45:49.076 --> 45:52.476 align:middle
so I feel he's very
well positioned

45:52.816 --> 45:56.696 align:middle
and very knowledgeable
of all the ins and outs

45:56.696 --> 45:58.666 align:middle
of everything that the Fed does.

45:58.946 --> 46:01.276 align:middle
In supervision, in
the payment system--

46:01.276 --> 46:05.266 align:middle
he's played an active role
there-- and in monetary policy,

46:05.266 --> 46:09.076 align:middle
and I have confidence
that he is very capable

46:09.076 --> 46:12.906 align:middle
of steering the Federal
Reserve in the years ahead.

46:14.666 --> 46:20.806 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

46:21.676 --> 46:28.386 align:middle
Do you mean me, or-- as long
as everyone gets a turn.

46:28.446 --> 46:30.346 align:middle
This is John Heltman
with the American Banker.

46:30.866 --> 46:34.776 align:middle
Sort of a related question
to what Nancy was asking--

46:35.146 --> 46:38.126 align:middle
there have been some
reservations among some

46:38.126 --> 46:42.666 align:middle
Democratic senators recently
during Governor Powell's

46:42.666 --> 46:44.476 align:middle
confirmation hearing
and during the markup

46:44.476 --> 46:49.216 align:middle
of the reg relief bill about
the approach towards regulation

46:49.456 --> 46:52.956 align:middle
that the new leadership
appears to be adopting.

46:52.956 --> 46:55.726 align:middle
Do you have any similar
reservations

46:55.766 --> 46:59.626 align:middle
about the new leadership's
ability to deregulate

46:59.626 --> 47:04.066 align:middle
in an evenhanded way that keeps
an eye towards the stability

47:04.066 --> 47:05.676 align:middle
of the financial
system and the--

47:05.676 --> 47:07.666 align:middle
sort of, the advances
that have come

47:07.666 --> 47:09.386 align:middle
from post-crisis regulation?

47:10.166 --> 47:10.676 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

47:10.796 --> 47:14.046 align:middle
So I would say that
all of my colleagues

47:14.046 --> 47:19.766 align:middle
on the Board have expressed
a strong commitment to keep

47:19.766 --> 47:21.856 align:middle
in place the core reforms

47:22.266 --> 47:26.616 align:middle
that have produced a
stronger financial system.

47:26.906 --> 47:31.476 align:middle
And I'm considering there
stronger capital, particularly

47:31.476 --> 47:34.796 align:middle
for the most systemic
institutions--

47:35.146 --> 47:41.026 align:middle
higher-quality capital,
stronger liquidity requirements,

47:41.386 --> 47:47.046 align:middle
a rigorous stress-testing
program, and resolution planning

47:47.046 --> 47:53.596 align:middle
that will make it more
possible to resolve a firm

47:53.596 --> 47:56.836 align:middle
that encounters significant
distress.

47:56.886 --> 48:01.926 align:middle
So I have heard broad-based
commitment to those things,

48:01.926 --> 48:03.496 align:middle
and I think they are the core

48:03.496 --> 48:06.086 align:middle
of the reforms we
have put in place.

48:06.196 --> 48:09.616 align:middle
I think all of us agree
that it is appropriate

48:09.716 --> 48:15.066 align:middle
to tailor regulatory
requirements in all

48:15.066 --> 48:19.706 align:middle
of those areas and others to
the systemic footprint of firms.

48:20.136 --> 48:23.666 align:middle
We have done a lot to do that,
and I think there is more

48:23.666 --> 48:27.696 align:middle
that could be done, and in
some areas it would require

48:27.696 --> 48:30.976 align:middle
legislation in order to do that.

48:31.236 --> 48:34.036 align:middle
But I believe all of
my colleagues and I are

48:34.036 --> 48:38.686 align:middle
in agreement on that, and we're
also focused on community banks

48:38.686 --> 48:42.916 align:middle
and want to find ways
to relieve burdens.

48:42.916 --> 48:48.166 align:middle
So in those important
ways, I do think that all

48:48.166 --> 48:51.266 align:middle
of my colleagues are
in the same place

48:51.266 --> 48:53.566 align:middle
with respect to their
priorities.

48:53.906 --> 48:54.446 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

48:54.446 --> 48:57.246 align:middle
Are there any important
differences

48:57.686 --> 49:02.796 align:middle
between your colleagues and your
vision on regulatory reform?

49:02.836 --> 49:03.376 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:03.706 --> 49:05.766 align:middle
So I have not seen anything
emerge at this point

49:06.716 --> 49:08.546 align:middle
that I would describe as
a significant difference.

49:08.676 --> 49:09.836 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

49:09.836 --> 49:11.376 align:middle
Hi, Howard Schneider
with Reuters.

49:11.736 --> 49:14.016 align:middle
So you mentioned in
response to Steve's question

49:14.016 --> 49:17.116 align:middle
that asset valuations, you
didn't think, were on the sort

49:17.116 --> 49:19.296 align:middle
of high-priority
risk list right now.

49:19.806 --> 49:22.136 align:middle
So I'm wondering, what do you
think is on that risk list?

49:22.256 --> 49:25.186 align:middle
And, more broadly, what
have you left undone?

49:25.186 --> 49:26.996 align:middle
You've gotten high marks

49:26.996 --> 49:30.596 align:middle
for bringing the economy
back towards its goals,

49:31.146 --> 49:33.906 align:middle
but are there things that are
going to nag you when you walk

49:33.906 --> 49:35.386 align:middle
out of here in February
and say, "Really,

49:35.386 --> 49:37.246 align:middle
I wish I'd seen this
to completion"?

49:37.246 --> 49:39.006 align:middle
I mean, we're not doing
negative interest rates.

49:39.006 --> 49:40.556 align:middle
We're not doing inflation
framework.

49:41.196 --> 49:44.996 align:middle
What's at your top of-- what's
at the top of the to-do list

49:45.046 --> 49:48.316 align:middle
that you are not getting to
see to bring to ground here?

49:48.786 --> 49:49.216 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:49.516 --> 49:51.446 align:middle
So you asked about
the risk list.

49:52.116 --> 49:55.826 align:middle
There are always risks
that affect the outlook.

49:56.206 --> 50:01.276 align:middle
We tend to focus in our own
evaluation on economic risks,

50:01.276 --> 50:03.476 align:middle
and we've characterized
them as "balanced."

50:03.896 --> 50:06.196 align:middle
And I think they are balanced.

50:07.056 --> 50:11.326 align:middle
You know, I can always give
you a list of, you know,

50:12.236 --> 50:15.966 align:middle
potential troubles,
international developments

50:15.966 --> 50:20.386 align:middle
that could result in
downside economic risk.

50:20.666 --> 50:25.546 align:middle
But, look, at the moment the
U.S. economy is performing well.

50:26.216 --> 50:31.656 align:middle
The growth that we're seeing,
it's not based on, for example,

50:31.656 --> 50:36.836 align:middle
an unsustainable buildup of
debt as we had in the run-up

50:36.836 --> 50:38.606 align:middle
to the financial crisis.

50:39.176 --> 50:43.316 align:middle
The global economy
is doing well.

50:43.316 --> 50:45.506 align:middle
We're in a synchronized
expansion.

50:45.506 --> 50:49.516 align:middle
This is the first time in many
years that we've seen this.

50:50.156 --> 50:53.956 align:middle
Inflation around the
world is generally low.

50:55.346 --> 50:59.706 align:middle
So I think the risks are
balanced, and there's less

50:59.736 --> 51:04.696 align:middle
to lose sleep about now than has
been true for quite some time,

51:04.696 --> 51:08.146 align:middle
so I feel good about
the economic out--

51:08.446 --> 51:10.006 align:middle
the economic outlook.

51:10.676 --> 51:15.306 align:middle
I feel, you know, good
that the labor market is

51:15.306 --> 51:21.216 align:middle
in a very much stronger place
than it was eight years ago.

51:21.216 --> 51:23.746 align:middle
We have created 17 million jobs.

51:23.746 --> 51:26.226 align:middle
We've got a good,
strong labor market

51:26.226 --> 51:28.886 align:middle
and a very low unemployment
rate,

51:29.336 --> 51:32.636 align:middle
and I think that's been
tremendously important

51:32.636 --> 51:37.266 align:middle
to the well-being of American
households and workers.

51:37.266 --> 51:46.456 align:middle
And I feel very pleased when
I hear anecdotes from firms

51:46.456 --> 51:49.416 align:middle
that tell me they're having
a hard time finding workers,

51:49.686 --> 51:53.716 align:middle
and they talk about, given
that they're taking on people

51:53.716 --> 51:57.346 align:middle
with skills that don't
quite match what they want,

51:57.416 --> 52:00.536 align:middle
but they're training
them, and, you know,

52:00.756 --> 52:03.046 align:middle
giving them the training
that they need in order

52:03.046 --> 52:04.606 align:middle
to be able to fill jobs.

52:04.866 --> 52:09.316 align:middle
I think that's a development
that is a natural one

52:09.316 --> 52:12.276 align:middle
that occurs in a strong
labor market that tends

52:12.276 --> 52:16.506 align:middle
to build human capital
and worker skills,

52:16.506 --> 52:19.236 align:middle
and that that's a
strong positive.

52:19.576 --> 52:23.446 align:middle
As I mentioned, I think
the financial system is

52:23.836 --> 52:28.456 align:middle
on much sounder footing, and
that we have done a great deal

52:28.566 --> 52:35.496 align:middle
to put in place greater
capital, liquidity, and so forth

52:35.496 --> 52:38.646 align:middle
that make it less crisis prone,

52:38.836 --> 52:42.486 align:middle
and that has been an
important objective.

52:42.766 --> 52:46.046 align:middle
What's on my "undone"
list, you ask?

52:47.186 --> 52:51.496 align:middle
We have a 2 percent symmetric
inflation objective, and,

52:51.496 --> 52:53.066 align:middle
for a number of years now,

52:53.256 --> 52:56.216 align:middle
inflation has been
running under 2 percent.

52:56.506 --> 53:00.186 align:middle
And I consider it an
important priority to make sure

53:00.186 --> 53:04.346 align:middle
that inflation doesn't
chronically undershoot our 2

53:04.346 --> 53:08.166 align:middle
percent objective, and I want
to see it move up to 2 percent.

53:08.166 --> 53:13.006 align:middle
So most of my colleagues and I
do believe that it's being held

53:13.006 --> 53:17.976 align:middle
down by transitory factors,
but there's work undone there,

53:17.976 --> 53:20.426 align:middle
in the sense-- we
need to see it move

53:20.426 --> 53:21.996 align:middle
up in line with our objective.

53:23.256 --> 53:24.226 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

53:29.456 --> 53:30.076 align:middle
Thank you.

53:30.076 --> 53:36.166 align:middle
Chair Yellen, what do you think
will be the drivers of inflation

53:36.536 --> 53:37.956 align:middle
over the next couple of years,

53:38.566 --> 53:42.726 align:middle
and how long will the Committee
go with low unemployment,

53:42.726 --> 53:46.516 align:middle
low inflation before you
rethink monetary policy--

53:47.496 --> 53:48.996 align:middle
this gradual rate hikes?

53:49.166 --> 53:50.066 align:middle
Thank you.

53:52.936 --> 53:53.546 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:54.336 --> 53:57.596 align:middle
So, you know, I think
for a number of years--

53:57.596 --> 54:01.836 align:middle
so we've had an undershoot of
inflation for a number of years.

54:01.836 --> 54:03.956 align:middle
We absolutely recognize that.

54:04.776 --> 54:10.376 align:middle
I think until this year,
undershoot was understandable.

54:10.906 --> 54:14.506 align:middle
First we had a good deal of
slack in the labor market.

54:14.946 --> 54:17.466 align:middle
Then we had plummeting
oil prices.

54:17.916 --> 54:22.506 align:middle
And, beginning in mid-2014,
there was a marked depreciation

54:22.506 --> 54:27.566 align:middle
in the dollar.2 And those three
factors held down inflation

54:27.566 --> 54:29.006 align:middle
for a number of years.

54:29.646 --> 54:35.876 align:middle
Now, in 2016, core inflation
came very close to 2 percent.

54:36.856 --> 54:40.316 align:middle
We seemed to be on a path
of inflation moving up,

54:40.686 --> 54:45.036 align:middle
and this year, beginning
in March, there seemed

54:45.036 --> 54:48.076 align:middle
to be a sequence of
negative surprises.

54:48.456 --> 54:56.406 align:middle
Some reflect one-time factors
that were easily identifiable,

54:56.406 --> 54:58.306 align:middle
like a marked decline

54:58.306 --> 55:02.426 align:middle
in quality-adjusted
cell phone plans.

55:02.856 --> 55:08.636 align:middle
There may be other factors
that are not so easy to name,

55:08.896 --> 55:10.566 align:middle
but we would judge-- you know,

55:10.566 --> 55:13.606 align:middle
inflation doesn't
always follow exactly.

55:13.796 --> 55:16.136 align:middle
There are errors
and many factors

55:16.136 --> 55:19.176 align:middle
that affect it beyond
the key influences

55:19.176 --> 55:22.996 align:middle
of labor market slack:
exchange rates

55:22.996 --> 55:25.426 align:middle
and import prices
and oil prices.

55:25.426 --> 55:26.956 align:middle
Those are three big ones.

55:26.956 --> 55:30.616 align:middle
But there are other factors
that affect inflation too,

55:30.616 --> 55:35.006 align:middle
and our judgment at this point
is that transitory factors

55:35.046 --> 55:36.516 align:middle
that are unrelated

55:36.516 --> 55:40.496 align:middle
to the broader macroeconomic
outlook are holding

55:40.496 --> 55:41.696 align:middle
inflation down.

55:42.106 --> 55:46.516 align:middle
But I have tried to be
straightforward in saying

55:46.706 --> 55:52.356 align:middle
that this could end up being
something that is more ingrained

55:52.356 --> 55:54.156 align:middle
and turns out to be permanent.

55:54.506 --> 56:00.256 align:middle
It's very important to
watch it and, if necessary,

56:00.346 --> 56:03.896 align:middle
rethink what's determining
inflation.

56:03.896 --> 56:07.846 align:middle
A possibility is that the
longer-run sustainable rate

56:07.846 --> 56:11.646 align:middle
of unemployment is--
it's been coming down.

56:11.646 --> 56:14.756 align:middle
Estimates in the
Committee have come down.

56:14.986 --> 56:18.016 align:middle
It's conceivable that they
need to come down even more.

56:18.056 --> 56:20.186 align:middle
It's not my judgment

56:20.186 --> 56:23.116 align:middle
that inflation expectations
have slipped,

56:23.386 --> 56:26.146 align:middle
but that also remains
a possibility

56:26.146 --> 56:27.766 align:middle
that needs to be monitored.

56:28.836 --> 56:33.656 align:middle
So there are-- you know, there
could be a rethink of inflation.

56:33.796 --> 56:38.666 align:middle
I think it's important to watch
inflation outcomes carefully

56:38.896 --> 56:42.446 align:middle
and, if we don't see
inflation moving in the manner

56:42.516 --> 56:47.576 align:middle
that the Committee
anticipates, to alter policy

56:47.576 --> 56:50.476 align:middle
so that we do achieve
our 2 percent objective.

56:50.586 --> 56:55.216 align:middle
But, at the moment, most of my
colleagues and I believe we are

56:55.216 --> 56:57.246 align:middle
on track to achieve it.

56:59.846 --> 57:00.686 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

57:01.356 --> 57:02.416 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen.

57:02.416 --> 57:04.186 align:middle
Jim Puzzanghera with
the Los Angeles Times.

57:04.616 --> 57:06.796 align:middle
I'm wondering, you
mentioned this is your last

57:06.796 --> 57:07.536 align:middle
news conference.

57:07.536 --> 57:09.986 align:middle
What are your plans when
you step down from the Fed?

57:09.986 --> 57:11.506 align:middle
Are you going to
remain in Washington?

57:11.836 --> 57:13.166 align:middle
Will you go back to Berkeley?

57:13.466 --> 57:16.736 align:middle
And, also, I'm curious if
you have any disappointment

57:17.236 --> 57:19.766 align:middle
that you're not going to be
continuing in this job as some

57:19.766 --> 57:21.096 align:middle
of your predecessors have done.

57:21.606 --> 57:22.416 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

57:22.416 --> 57:25.516 align:middle
So, on my plans, I don't
have anything definite

57:25.516 --> 57:26.916 align:middle
for you, I guess.

57:26.986 --> 57:30.176 align:middle
I have a home in Berkeley
and expect to maintain it.

57:30.236 --> 57:33.726 align:middle
But I would say, my
spouse is on the faculty--

57:33.726 --> 57:38.396 align:middle
he's a professor at Georgetown
and would like to stay

57:38.396 --> 57:43.336 align:middle
in his job, and we expect to
maintain Washington as our base.

57:43.336 --> 57:46.856 align:middle
But I don't have
any definite plans.

57:48.906 --> 57:49.976 align:middle
Let's see.

57:50.356 --> 57:52.616 align:middle
And you asked me if
I'm disappointed.

57:53.136 --> 57:59.026 align:middle
So let me just say that I have
served in senior positions

57:59.286 --> 58:02.386 align:middle
in the Federal Reserve
now for quite a long time.

58:02.886 --> 58:07.716 align:middle
I became president in
San Francisco in 2004,

58:08.116 --> 58:13.756 align:middle
and I've participated in
the FOMC since that time

58:13.756 --> 58:16.606 align:middle
as president, as Vice
Chair, and as Chair,

58:16.916 --> 58:21.256 align:middle
and it's been an immensely
rewarding experience for me.

58:21.626 --> 58:29.836 align:middle
I feel very positive about what
we've been able to accomplish

58:30.216 --> 58:36.686 align:middle
and feel tremendous, you know,
loyalty to the institution.

58:37.106 --> 58:42.046 align:middle
So I did make the judgment that
this is the right time for me

58:42.046 --> 58:46.316 align:middle
to leave, but I feel I have
served in senior positions

58:46.546 --> 58:50.126 align:middle
at the Fed for a long time,
and it's really been an honor

58:50.126 --> 58:53.006 align:middle
and a privilege for me to
have had a chance to do so.

58:53.536 --> 58:55.636 align:middle
ADAM SHAPIRO.

58:59.566 --> 59:01.196 align:middle
Adam Shapiro with Fox Business.

59:01.476 --> 59:03.006 align:middle
Glad to hear you're
staying on the East Coast.

59:03.606 --> 59:04.796 align:middle
I'm just curious,
I wanted to follow

59:04.796 --> 59:06.666 align:middle
up to the bitcoin
questions, but--

59:06.666 --> 59:10.116 align:middle
because you've lived through
what we all experienced in 2007,

59:10.116 --> 59:14.956 align:middle
2008, and-- should the Fed take
a more active role in trying

59:14.956 --> 59:17.676 align:middle
to identify who some
of the counterparty

59:17.676 --> 59:21.226 align:middle
and what the exposure is with
bitcoin as a potential threat

59:21.226 --> 59:22.526 align:middle
to financial stability?

59:22.526 --> 59:25.586 align:middle
Just-- the best minds at the Fed
can sometimes miss these kinds

59:25.586 --> 59:26.036 align:middle
of threats.

59:26.256 --> 59:28.886 align:middle
Your predecessor, Mr.
Bernanke, actually said in '07

59:29.376 --> 59:32.426 align:middle
that the contagion from subprime
would not affect the housing

59:32.426 --> 59:33.486 align:middle
market or the economy.

59:33.836 --> 59:37.486 align:middle
So are we underestimating the
potential threat from bitcoin

59:37.486 --> 59:38.886 align:middle
as it runs up in value?

59:38.886 --> 59:39.186 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

59:39.976 --> 59:44.766 align:middle
Well, I certainly agree that
it's important for the Fed

59:45.326 --> 59:51.046 align:middle
to attempt to understand
emerging risks

59:51.076 --> 59:56.076 align:middle
to financial stability
and to be looking not just

59:56.076 --> 01:00:00.756 align:middle
in the banking system but
outside it for developments

01:00:00.756 --> 01:00:05.706 align:middle
that could pose financial
risks, and we are doing that.

01:00:05.706 --> 01:00:10.586 align:middle
And, I would say, one of the
changes during my tenure is,

01:00:10.896 --> 01:00:15.756 align:middle
we devoted-- decided in
the aftermath of the crisis

01:00:15.756 --> 01:00:19.626 align:middle
that we needed to devote
considerable resources

01:00:19.916 --> 01:00:25.046 align:middle
to financial stability to
monitor for emerging threats,

01:00:25.286 --> 01:00:28.926 align:middle
and now we have at the
Board a full-blown division

01:00:29.186 --> 01:00:32.876 align:middle
of financial stability that's
involved in doing that.

01:00:33.486 --> 01:00:36.806 align:middle
Now, when you ask about bitcoin,

01:00:37.016 --> 01:00:41.266 align:middle
I still see the financial
stability risks

01:00:41.266 --> 01:00:43.606 align:middle
from it as limited.

01:00:45.476 --> 01:00:50.186 align:middle
Often, risks threatening
financial stability arise

01:00:50.456 --> 01:00:53.436 align:middle
when there's exposure
of the banking system

01:00:54.496 --> 01:00:57.436 align:middle
to fluctuating asset valuations,

01:00:57.906 --> 01:01:04.226 align:middle
and I really don't see
any significant exposure

01:01:04.226 --> 01:01:10.256 align:middle
of our core financial
institutions to threats

01:01:10.256 --> 01:01:13.226 align:middle
from bitcoin if its
value were to fluctuate.

01:01:13.696 --> 01:01:17.376 align:middle
I don't see a threat to our
core financial institutions.

01:01:17.696 --> 01:01:22.176 align:middle
So, undoubtedly, there are
individuals who could lose a lot

01:01:22.176 --> 01:01:27.476 align:middle
of money if bitcoin were to fall
in price, but I really don't see

01:01:27.476 --> 01:01:32.026 align:middle
that as creating a full-blown
financial stability risk.

01:01:33.116 --> 01:01:34.176 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

01:01:35.776 --> 01:01:37.796 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

01:01:37.796 --> 01:01:40.426 align:middle
I suppose I should be asking
you a valedictory question

01:01:40.426 --> 01:01:41.536 align:middle
since it's the last question,

01:01:41.536 --> 01:01:43.376 align:middle
but I don't think you can
top what you've already said,

01:01:43.376 --> 01:01:46.276 align:middle
so let me just do a couple
of cleanup questions here.

01:01:46.636 --> 01:01:48.256 align:middle
President Trump, while
you were speaking,

01:01:48.256 --> 01:01:50.846 align:middle
just said that he thinks
his tax plan will produce 4

01:01:50.846 --> 01:01:51.546 align:middle
percent growth.

01:01:52.116 --> 01:01:53.836 align:middle
Do you think that is possible?

01:01:54.366 --> 01:01:59.356 align:middle
Second, do you think that there
is any Fed blame or complicity

01:01:59.386 --> 01:02:01.506 align:middle
in the flattening of the yield
curve, and are you worried

01:02:01.796 --> 01:02:04.886 align:middle
that there might be some sort of
policy mistake built into that

01:02:05.176 --> 01:02:06.486 align:middle
that could slow the economy?

01:02:06.786 --> 01:02:09.676 align:middle
And the last question, which
is a bit of a valedictory,

01:02:09.876 --> 01:02:11.866 align:middle
is one that everybody on
Wall Street has wanted

01:02:11.866 --> 01:02:13.116 align:middle
to ask you for four years.

01:02:13.436 --> 01:02:15.146 align:middle
Since this is your
last press conference,

01:02:15.366 --> 01:02:17.266 align:middle
can you tell us which
dot is yours?

01:02:17.626 --> 01:02:18.326 align:middle
[Laughter] CHAIR YELLEN.

01:02:19.326 --> 01:02:22.366 align:middle
Well, I can answer the
last question first.

01:02:22.476 --> 01:02:23.906 align:middle
The answer is "no."

01:02:23.906 --> 01:02:26.706 align:middle
I've never been willing to
reveal which dot is mine,

01:02:26.706 --> 01:02:30.446 align:middle
and I'm not going
to change that now.

01:02:30.776 --> 01:02:34.856 align:middle
So, you know, my assessment,

01:02:34.856 --> 01:02:37.906 align:middle
and I think most participants'
assessments, as I said,

01:02:38.246 --> 01:02:44.576 align:middle
of the impact of the tax policy
on growth has been informed

01:02:44.576 --> 01:02:50.316 align:middle
by work by the Joint Committee
on Taxation and other analysts.

01:02:50.356 --> 01:02:54.586 align:middle
And everyone recognizes
that there's uncertainty

01:02:54.586 --> 01:02:57.426 align:middle
about what the economic
effects would be,

01:02:57.656 --> 01:02:59.686 align:middle
and I wouldn't want
to rule anything out.

01:02:59.996 --> 01:03:04.446 align:middle
It is challenging,
however, to achieve growth

01:03:04.446 --> 01:03:07.846 align:middle
of the levels that
you mentioned.

01:03:08.086 --> 01:03:12.616 align:middle
Look, if the package
were to stimulate growth

01:03:12.616 --> 01:03:15.296 align:middle
of that magnitude,
let me just say again,

01:03:15.296 --> 01:03:17.696 align:middle
the Federal Reserve
would welcome that.

01:03:17.966 --> 01:03:19.586 align:middle
If it's a supply-side--

01:03:19.646 --> 01:03:22.516 align:middle
favorable supply-side
developments

01:03:22.616 --> 01:03:28.156 align:middle
that would be compatible with
the attainment of our employment

01:03:28.156 --> 01:03:31.736 align:middle
and inflation objectives,
that's something

01:03:31.736 --> 01:03:35.316 align:middle
that would be very,
very welcome.

01:03:35.316 --> 01:03:38.676 align:middle
But it would be challenging
to achieve numbers like that.

01:03:38.676 --> 01:03:43.626 align:middle
Let's see, I think you also then
asked me about the yield curve,

01:03:43.626 --> 01:03:46.116 align:middle
and-- I mean, there
is much discussion

01:03:46.116 --> 01:03:49.196 align:middle
about yield curve
inversions and whether

01:03:49.196 --> 01:03:53.146 align:middle
or not a flattening yield
curve could signal a recession.

01:03:53.146 --> 01:03:55.806 align:middle
Is that the brunt
of your question?

01:03:55.806 --> 01:03:56.236 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

01:03:56.286 --> 01:03:57.646 align:middle
And whether the Fed has made--

01:03:57.756 --> 01:03:59.626 align:middle
if there's a policy
mistake embedded in that.

01:04:00.436 --> 01:04:01.006 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

01:04:01.006 --> 01:04:04.756 align:middle
So this is something that we
discussed and have looked at.

01:04:05.116 --> 01:04:08.096 align:middle
The yield curve has
flattened some

01:04:08.096 --> 01:04:09.616 align:middle
as we have raised short rates.

01:04:09.616 --> 01:04:13.886 align:middle
It mainly-- the flattening yield
curve mainly reflects higher

01:04:13.886 --> 01:04:15.136 align:middle
short-term rates.

01:04:15.626 --> 01:04:20.316 align:middle
The yield curve is not currently
inverted, and I would say

01:04:20.316 --> 01:04:26.616 align:middle
that the current slope is well
within its historical range.

01:04:26.936 --> 01:04:29.876 align:middle
Now, there is a strong
correlation historically

01:04:30.266 --> 01:04:33.716 align:middle
between yield curve
inversions and recessions,

01:04:34.056 --> 01:04:38.406 align:middle
but let me emphasize that
correlation is not causation,

01:04:38.786 --> 01:04:41.636 align:middle
and I think that there
are good reasons to think

01:04:42.066 --> 01:04:45.286 align:middle
that the relationship between
the slope of the yield curve

01:04:45.566 --> 01:04:48.396 align:middle
and the business cycle
may have changed.

01:04:48.816 --> 01:04:52.976 align:middle
And one reason for that is

01:04:52.976 --> 01:04:57.526 align:middle
that long-term interest rates
generally embody two factors.

01:04:57.786 --> 01:05:02.846 align:middle
One is the expected average
value of short rates over, say,

01:05:02.896 --> 01:05:05.516 align:middle
10 years, and the second piece

01:05:05.516 --> 01:05:08.296 align:middle
of it is a so-called
term premium

01:05:08.606 --> 01:05:13.856 align:middle
that often reflects things like
inflation-- inflation risk.

01:05:15.376 --> 01:05:20.526 align:middle
Typically, the term premium
historically has been positive.

01:05:21.246 --> 01:05:25.146 align:middle
So when the yield curve
has inverted historically,

01:05:25.966 --> 01:05:29.296 align:middle
it meant that short-term
rates were well

01:05:29.296 --> 01:05:34.176 align:middle
above average expected short
rates over the longer run.

01:05:34.456 --> 01:05:37.616 align:middle
So with the positive term
premium, that's what it means.

01:05:38.066 --> 01:05:42.996 align:middle
And typically that means that
monetary policy is restrictive,

01:05:42.996 --> 01:05:44.806 align:middle
sometimes quite restrictive,

01:05:45.196 --> 01:05:48.146 align:middle
and some of those
recessions were situations

01:05:48.146 --> 01:05:52.896 align:middle
in which the Fed was consciously
tightening monetary policy

01:05:53.216 --> 01:05:57.186 align:middle
because inflation was high and
trying to slow the economy.

01:05:57.646 --> 01:06:02.646 align:middle
Well, right now the term premium
is estimated to be quite low,

01:06:02.646 --> 01:06:06.566 align:middle
close to zero, and that
means that, structurally--

01:06:06.566 --> 01:06:08.496 align:middle
and this can be true
going forward--

01:06:08.786 --> 01:06:11.816 align:middle
that the yield curve
is likely to be flatter

01:06:11.916 --> 01:06:13.436 align:middle
than it's been in the past.

01:06:13.786 --> 01:06:17.046 align:middle
And so it could more
easily invert.

01:06:17.406 --> 01:06:19.166 align:middle
If the Fed were to even move

01:06:19.166 --> 01:06:22.646 align:middle
to a slightly restrictive
policy stance,

01:06:22.866 --> 01:06:26.836 align:middle
you could see an inversion
with a zero term premium.

01:06:27.416 --> 01:06:33.456 align:middle
So I think the fact the
term premium is so low

01:06:33.456 --> 01:06:38.296 align:middle
and the yield curve is generally
flatter is an important factor

01:06:38.296 --> 01:06:39.166 align:middle
to consider.

01:06:39.166 --> 01:06:41.976 align:middle
Now, I think it's also
important to realize

01:06:41.976 --> 01:06:47.006 align:middle
that market participants are not
expressing heightened concern

01:06:47.366 --> 01:06:50.346 align:middle
about the decline of
the term premium, and,

01:06:50.566 --> 01:06:53.626 align:middle
when asked directly about
the odds of recession,

01:06:54.446 --> 01:06:57.976 align:middle
they see it as low, and I would
concur with that judgment.

01:06:58.456 --> 01:07:02.746 align:middle
Thanks very much.

